  "strategy": "A substantively different approach to the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem involves Bayesian optimization with adaptive resolution expansion, incorporating trust-region strategies and variance-aware exploration. This method moves beyond local search and relies on probabilistic modeling of the objective function landscape, enabling jumps between several fixed resolutions while maintaining mathematical guarantees.\n\n1. Approach and Rationale:\nThe central idea is to:\n- Model the overlap integral objective with Bayesian methods\n- Use multi-resolution expansion informed by exploration vs. exploitation metrics\n- Integrate trust-region adaptation within hierarchical resolution levels\n- Preserve constraint feasibility through active constraint handling\n- Maintain parallel fitness evaluation in a portfolio of candidate solutions\n\nThis differs significantly from previous submissions by:\n- Employing probabilistic optimization techniques (Bayesian/Expectation Propagation)\n- Using adaptive trust-region mechanisms (Nelder-Mead on reduced-dimension spaces)\n- Incorporating active-set methods for constraint handling\n- Maintaining formal optimality certificates through finite-difference linearization\n\nKey domain considerations include:\n- The objective is non-convex with many local minima\n- Complete preservation of integral/dimension constraints is essential\n- Finite-difference evaluation must be accurate for numerical second-order methods\n- The mapping from discrete grid functions to continuous overlap integrals requires careful normalization\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\nDefine optimization variables: grid samples h\u2081...\u2099 \u2208 [0,1] with \u2211h\u1d62 = n/2\nObjective: minimize max\u2096 integral of h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\nFinite-difference evaluation: use central differences for gradient approximation (n\u2264240 grid points)\nTrust-region filter method: \n  - Constraint handling via logarithmic barrier functions\n  - Model management with expectation propagation\n  - Optimization method: Truncated BFGS on reduced space\nConstraint representation:\n  - Box constraint barrier: \u03c8_box = \u2211\u2083\u1d62\u208c\u2081 (-(1/\u03b5) * ln(1-(x\u1d62-0)/1))\n  - Integral constraint: \u2211h\u1d62 = n/2 \u21d2 \u2211(h\u1d62 - h\u1d62 target) = 0\nAugmented Lagrangian formalism for integral constraint enforcement:\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\nDefine nested procedures:\n- `evaluate_fitness(h, n_points)` - exact evaluation via FFT (prune zero-lag noise first)\n- `project_down(h)` - piecewise constant projection to lower resolution\n- `mcmc_restart(embedded_dim)` - Bayesian optimization with dimension embedding\n- `trust_region_optimizer(x0, bounds, model)` - quadratic model optimization\n\nAlgorithm structure:\n1. Initial setup:\n   - Seed from `initial_h_values` (n\u2080=45)\n   - Validate initial feasibility/equivalence harmonic\n2. Bayesian exploration cycle:\n   - Parameter sample with dimension embedding \u226540\n   - Surrogate model training with EP/EPOMP for marginalization\n   - Expected improvement selection\n   - Trust-region optimization with simplified dimensionality\n   - Dimension-adaptive expansion based on score history\n\n4. Search Choices:\n- Target resolutions: 45, 90, 180, 360\n- Bayesian restart probability: 2% per function eval\n- Expected improvement value: 1e-4 to 1e-6\n- Maximum evaluations per resolution: 20-50 (depending on grid)\n- Dimension embedding map: find k such that k < \u03c4(grid) + log(grid) < 1.5\u03c4(grid)\n\n5. Compute Budget:\n- Total evaluations: individually time-calibrated for grid sizes 45, 90, 180, 360\n- Conservative estimate: 500 evaluations per resolution level\n- Estimated run time: 1000-2000 seconds (parallelized cores assumed)\n- Wall-clock monitoring for estimation\n\n6. Validation and Contract:\n- Post-run checks:\n  - 0\u2264h[i]\u22641 verified via sample\n  - \u2211h[i] = n_points/2 (exact integer, not scaling)\n  - Overlap calculation via unidirectional FFT\n  - Five-digit floating point precision\n\nEventual return:`(h_current, c5_score, n_points)`\n\nThe implementation specifies full closed-form transformations, dimension-consistent tensor operations, and formal derivative handling. All numerical operations are subject to finite-difference verification and stepped debugging cycles.",
